  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 258 AM MST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nGetting over the hump into late next week, we find ourselves under a \nweak ridge between two longwave troughs one to the east over the \nGreat Lakes and the other moving onshore over California. The ridge \nwill be brief and bring high pressure with subsidence at the surface \nwhich should help temperatures recover into the 60s on Friday. \nSpeaking of Friday, the aforementioned trough over the western US \nbecomes relevant for our area. Guidance projects it to take more of \na northeast track along the jetstream. An upper-level jet with \nsouthwest winds aloft is to come from this at the 300-500mb levels. \nDown at the surface, a low-pressure system and cold front forms and \ninfluences our coverage area. This brings to light our next legit \nchance of precipitation. After this system passes north of us late \nSaturday, winds become zonal as another modest ridge propagates \ntowards the county Warning Area (CWA). Winds aloft then shift back \nto the southwest as another trough looms to the west.\n\nAs mentioned above, our first shot at precip is in store late Friday \ninto Saturday morning. With a low off to the north/northeast and an \nassociated cold front. It is expected to be wet across the CWA with \ndewpoints in the 40s/low 50 so ample moisture for sure. Despite \neverything in place for a decent rain event, guidance is still not \nvery confident on a good steady rain. PoPs in the 30-50% range is \nforecasted so at least some rain should occur. Any rain that does \nfall shouldn't accumulate to much as QPF and other accumulations \nestimate in the range of 0.01 to 0.05. Therefore, there is still low \nconfidence we get anything more than light rain showers. Trends will \nbe monitored with this as the week goes on.\n\nHigh temperatures will be quite nice for this time of the year. \nAbove average highs are likely across the board through Friday. \nEnsembles agree quite well on seasonably warm highs with upper \n50/60s expected. There's a slight chance (30-40%) that it reaches 70 \ndegrees for parts of the CWA on Friday before that the front drops \ntemperatures quite a bit. Saturday and Sunday's post-frontal temps \nshould be much more normal for this time of the year, sitting around \n50 to 55 degrees. Overnight lows should vary quite a bit from west \nto east on Friday as the slow moving cold front advances eastward. \nMorning lows in the 40s are anticipated east of Highway 27 and mid \nto upper 30s west of there. Post-front, lows in 30s across the \nentire CWA is in play. After that, radiational cooling should lead \nto our coldest night of the season so far going into Sunday with \nlows expected in the 20s pretty much everywhere. Some locations may \nget as cold as 20 degrees on Sunday morning.\n\nFire weather remains a non-factor across the Tri-State area. \nRelative Humidity (RH) should be in the 40-70% range for the entire \nforecast period as a slew of relatively wet air masses drift through \nthe region. RHs should not fall below 30% at all but if they do, it \nwould be in a few spots throughout eastern Colorado. The driest day \nwill be Saturday but again, not concerned at all with GFDI values \nwell below fire weather criteria. It will likely be breezy through \nthe weekend with surface winds consistently from the south at around \n20-30 mph. Friday should be the windiest day with sustained winds up \nto 40 mph and gusting up to 55 mph. Winds then die down quite a bit \ngoing into next weekend as just 5-20 mph is forecast through Sunday.",
